<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-differential-equations">

  <title>Differential Equations</title>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-differential-equations-1">
    <title>Equilibria and Stability</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the differential equation
        <m>
          \frac{dy}{dt}=y-y^3.
        </m>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all equilibria. By calculating the eigenvalue at each
          equilibrium, determine which equilibria are stable and which
          are unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Equilibria satisfy
          <m>y-y^3=0</m>, so
        </p>

        <me>
          y(1-y^2)=0.
        </me>

        <p>
          Hence the equilibria are
          <m>y=-1,0,1</m>.
          Let <m>f(y)=y-y^3</m>. Then
          <m>f'(y)=1-3y^2</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Since
          <m>f'(-1)=-2</m>,
          <m>f'(0)=1</m>, and
          <m>f'(1)=-2</m>,
          the equilibria <m>y=-1</m> and <m>y=1</m> are stable,
          while <m>y=0</m> is unstable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the phase line for the differential equation.
          Use it to verify your stability classifications.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The sign of <m>y-y^3</m> is positive for
          <m>y&lt;-1</m> and <m>0&lt;y&lt;1</m>,
          and negative for
          <m>-1&lt;y&lt;0</m> and <m>y&gt;1</m>.
          Thus the arrows point toward <m>y=\pm1</m> and away from
          <m>y=0</m>, confirming the stability classifications.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given the initial condition <m>y(0)=0.5</m>, determine
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}y(t)</m>.
          You do not need to solve the differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}y(t)=1.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given the initial condition <m>y(0)=-0.5</m>, determine
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}y(t)</m>.
          You do not need to solve the differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}y(t)=-1.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-differential-equations-2">
    <title>The Logistic Model</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The logistic model for population growth is
      </p>

      <me>
        \frac{dN}{dt}
        =
        rN\left(1-\frac{N}{K}\right),
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>r&gt;0</m> is the per-capita growth rate and
        <m>K&gt;0</m> is the carrying capacity.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all equilibria of the model. By calculating the eigenvalue
          at each equilibrium, determine which equilibria are stable and
          which are unstable.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          The equilibria satisfy
        </p>

        <me>
          rN\left(1-\frac{N}{K}\right)=0,
        </me>

        <p>
          so <m>N=0</m> and <m>N=K</m>.
          Let
          <m>f(N)=rN(1-N/K)</m>. Then
        </p>

        <me>
          f'(N)=r-\frac{2r}{K}N.
        </me>

        <p>
          Since <m>f'(0)=r&gt;0</m>,
          <m>N=0</m> is unstable.
          Since <m>f'(K)=-r&lt;0</m>,
          <m>N=K</m> is stable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the phase line for the differential equation.
          Use it to verify your stability classifications.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          For <m>0&lt;N&lt;K</m>, the growth rate is positive, while
          for <m>N&gt;K</m>, it is negative. Thus solutions move away
          from <m>N=0</m> and toward <m>N=K</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given the initial condition <m>N(0)=K/2</m>, determine
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)</m>.
          You do not need to solve the differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)=K.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given the initial condition <m>N(0)=K+1</m>, determine
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)</m>.
          You do not need to solve the differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)=K.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-differential-equations-3">
    <title>Nutrient Dynamics in a Lake</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        A lake receives nutrient runoff from nearby farmland at a constant
        rate. The mass of nutrient in the lake at time <m>t</m>, denoted
        <m>m(t)</m> and measured in kilograms, satisfies
      </p>

      <me>
        \frac{dm}{dt}=r-km,
      </me>

      <p>
        where <m>r&gt;0</m> is the rate at which nutrient enters the lake,
        measured in kilograms per year, and <m>k&gt;0</m> is the fraction of
        nutrient removed from the lake per year.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assuming <m>m(0)=0</m>, solve the differential equation to find
          <m>m(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{dm}{r-km}=dt.
        </me>

        <p>
          Integrating yields
        </p>

        <me>
          -\frac{1}{k}\ln|r-km|=t+C.
        </me>

        <p>
          Solving for <m>m(t)</m> and using <m>m(0)=0</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
          m(t)=\frac{r}{k}\left(1-e^{-kt}\right).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}m(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}m(t)=\frac{r}{k}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose nutrient enters the lake at a rate of
          <m>r=5</m> kilograms per year and that
          <m>m(t)\to 25</m> kilograms as <m>t\to\infty</m>.
          Calculate <m>k</m> and include units.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Since the limiting mass is <m>r/k</m>,
        </p>

        <me>
          25=\frac{5}{k}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Therefore,
        </p>

        <me>
          k=\frac{1}{5}\text{ year}^{-1}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>


  <exercise xml:id="practice-differential-equations-4">
    <title>von Bertalanffy Fish Growth</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>L(t)</m> denote the length of a fish at time <m>t</m>.
        Suppose the fish grows according to
      </p>

      <me>
        \frac{dL}{dt}=k(28-L(t)),
        \qquad
        L(0)=4.
      </me>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the differential equation.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
        </p>

        <me>
          \frac{dL}{28-L}=k\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
          Integrating and solving for <m>L</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
          L(t)=28-Ae^{-kt}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Using <m>L(0)=4</m> gives <m>A=24</m>, so
        </p>

        <me>
          L(t)=28-24e^{-kt}.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use your solution to determine <m>k</m> under the assumption
          that <m>L(4)=12</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Substituting <m>L(4)=12</m> gives
        </p>

        <me>
          12=28-24e^{-4k}.
        </me>

        <p>
          Thus
          <m>e^{-4k}=2/3</m>, and therefore
        </p>

        <me>
          k=\frac{1}{4}\ln\left(\frac{3}{2}\right).
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the asymptotic length of the fish; that is, determine
          <m>\lim_{t\to\infty}L(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <me>
          \lim_{t\to\infty}L(t)=28.
        </me>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exercise>

</section>